        "prompt": "Profile the immutable evaluated round 1 (round_num=1) for the selu_ in-place elementwise Triton kernel on Ascend910B, then record and persist a validated analysis and return exactly one actionable next experiment.\n\nContext: round 1 is the baseline (BLOCK_SIZE=1024, one program per 1024 elements, launched via `triton` kernel with a lambda grid and a `with flag_gems.runtime.torch_device_fn.device(x.device)` context). Measured geo_mean speedup was only 0.130 vs the torch.selu_ reference. Notably:\n- 1024-element workloads: candidate 0.075 ms vs reference 0.0116 ms (~6.5x slower) — the candidate has a fixed per-call overhead of roughly 60 us above reference, suggesting host-side launch overhead.\n- 4096x4096 workloads: candidate 0.60 ms vs reference 0.049-0.057 ms (~10x slower), i.e. ~111 GB/s effective; the 33.5 MB buffer fits in the 176 MB L2, so the reference may be L2-resident.\n- 65536 and 1048576 element workloads: candidate ~0.075 ms, identical to the tiny workloads, which again looks like a fixed floor.\n\nUse timings and as many timing workloads as are representative (the 1024-element fp16, the 1048576-element fp16, and the 4096x4096 fp32 or fp16 case would be the most informative) to determine, with backend-native evidence:\n1. How much of the measured walltime is kernel execution vs host-side/launch gap per invocation.\n2. Whether the 4096x4096 kernel is limited by MTE/memory bandwidth, by vector compute (exp), or by having far too many small grid tasks (16384 programs of 1024 elements on ~20 AI cores).\n3. What block size / programs-per-core granularity the hardware evidence favors.\n\nPersist the analysis with the profile tool. Report back a concise summary plus exactly one concrete next experiment (with concrete parameter values and expected effect), and explicitly state the evidence path(s) you used."
      }
